\begin{figure*}
\begin{center}
<<IMG_114>>
\caption{Illustrations of structure (left) and electron localization function plot (right) of the conventional cell of: (a) <<MATH_INLINE_197>> <<MATH_INLINE_198>>H<<MATH_INLINE_199>> and (b) <<MATH_INLINE_200>> <<MATH_INLINE_201>>H<<MATH_INLINE_202>> phases that contain electropositive metals, <<MATH_INLINE_203>>, with varying formal oxidation states: (a) KC<<MATH_INLINE_204>>H<<MATH_INLINE_205>>  (+1), (b) CaC<<MATH_INLINE_206>>H<<MATH_INLINE_207>>  (+2), (c) ScC<<MATH_INLINE_208>>H<<MATH_INLINE_209>>  (+3), (d) KB<<MATH_INLINE_210>>H<<MATH_INLINE_211>>  (+1), (e) BaB<<MATH_INLINE_212>>H<<MATH_INLINE_213>>  (+2), and (f) YB<<MATH_INLINE_214>>H<<MATH_INLINE_215>>  (+3). The top of the valence band (e) or Fermi energy (elsewhere) is set to 0~eV.}
    <<CMD_520>>
    \end{center}
\end{figure*}

CH<<MATH_INLINE_218>> is a neutral molecule, methane. When combined with a metal intercalant, as in the <<MATH_INLINE_219>>C<<MATH_INLINE_220>>H<<MATH_INLINE_221>> phases, charge is transferred from the electropositive metal atoms to methane-based anti-bonding bands, rendering CH<<MATH_INLINE_222>> slightly anionic.  Nevertheless, the CH<<MATH_INLINE_223>> units are predicted to be dynamically stable even to high pressures (ca.\ 300~GPa) in some of the studied compounds (Figure <<CMD_507>>).  
To highlight some of the noted trends, band structures and DOS plots of select structures are provided in Figure <<CMD_508>>. In KC<<MATH_INLINE_226>>H<<MATH_INLINE_227>> <<MATH_INLINE_228>> lies at the top of a peak in a feature of the DOS that corresponds to a half-filled band, resembling the electronic structure of <<MATH_INLINE_229>> Mg<<MATH_INLINE_230>>IrH<<MATH_INLINE_231>>, which was predicted to have a <<MATH_INLINE_232>> of 160-175~K at ambient pressure~<<CMD_485>>. Full charge transfer from the potassium would result in an excess of 0.5 electrons on each methane molecule, suggesting the presence of a quarter filled doubly degenerate band crossing <<MATH_INLINE_233>>. The band structure we calculate, however, is markedly different. To better understand the origin of the metallicity of this phase, we note that the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of methane, of <<MATH_INLINE_234>> symmetry, is mainly derived from an out-of-phase combination of the carbon and hydrogen <<MATH_INLINE_235>>-orbitals. This orbital is very diffuse, Rydberg-like, with a node near the hydrogen atoms, stemming from the contribution of higher lying orbitals. 

The LUMO of ammonia has a qualitatively similar appearance. A detailed quantum mechanical study on (NH<<MATH_INLINE_236>>)<<MATH_INLINE_237>> clusters showed that the overlap of the partially occupied LUMOs of the ammonia molecules results in a new type of bonding, denoted as H<<MATH_INLINE_238>>H bonding~<<CMD_486>>. In fact, such H<<MATH_INLINE_239>>H bonding, this time stemming from the overlap of the Li(NH<<MATH_INLINE_240>>)<<MATH_INLINE_241>> singly occupied molecular orbitals (SOMOs) in lithium(0)tetraamine solid, which also possess the same Rydberg-like characteristics, leads to a build up of charge in the interstices between molecules rendering this system an electride~<<CMD_487>>.  It turns out that the peculiar electronic structure of KC<<MATH_INLINE_242>>H<<MATH_INLINE_243>> can be explained by this same mechanism. Within it the 4b Wyckoff position is surrounded by eight hydrogen atoms from nearby methane molecules. As charge is transferred to the CH<<MATH_INLINE_244>> units their diffuse LUMOs overlap forming weak, but numerous H<<MATH_INLINE_245>>H interactions, leading to a build-up of charge between them. Indeed, an isosurface and contour plot of the  charge density associated with the filled portion of the metallic band illustrates that it corresponds to an interstitial blob, characteristic of an electride phase (Figure <<CMD_509>>).  There is one such blob per formula unit, corresponding to a single band, which is half-filled via transfer from the electropositive K atom. The H-to-blob-center distances measure 2.02~\AA{} at 4~GPa, decreasing to 1.24~\AA{} at 300~GPa. Curiously, the position of the blob also corresponds to the location of the beryllium atom in LaBeH<<MATH_INLINE_246>> (and the <<MATH_INLINE_247>>-block elements in related phases) -- in line with expectations based upon chemical template theory~<<CMD_488>>. CaC<<MATH_INLINE_248>>H<<MATH_INLINE_249>> (Figure <<CMD_510>>b) is a semi-metal resulting from the filling of this cavity-centered band. In ScC<<MATH_INLINE_252>>H<<MATH_INLINE_253>> (Figure <<CMD_511>>c) the Fermi level is shifted higher yet, and another set of bands crosses <<MATH_INLINE_256>>, this time with substantial scandium <<MATH_INLINE_257>>-character. 

